        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00012",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00012-alt",
              "text": "An alkyl halide reacts with sodium cyanide to form cyano nitrile. This reacts with lithium aluminum hydride in ether, then water to form an amine."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_001.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00013",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00013-alt",
              "text": "An amide reacts with lithium aluminum hydride in ether, then water to form an amine."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00014",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00014-alt",
              "text": "Nitrobenzene reacts with hydrogen, platinum, or iron, hydronium or tin (II) chloride, hydronium ion to form aniline."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00015",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00015-alt",
              "text": "The primary, secondary, and tertiary amine and quaternary ammonium salt are formed from ammonia, primary, secondary, and tertiary amine, respectively."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00016",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00016-alt",
              "text": "Phthalimide reacts with potassium hydroxide, then alkyl halide to form a product in which N-H gets replaced by N-R bond. This reacts with sodium hydroxide and water forming primary amine."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_005.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00017",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00017-alt",
              "text": "An alkyl halide reacts with sodium azide and ethanol to form a compound that further reacts with lithium aluminum hydride in ether, then water to form an amine."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_006.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00018",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00018-alt",
              "text": "A carbonyl compound reacts with ammonia and sodium borohydride to form a secondary amine."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_007.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00019",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00019-alt",
              "text": "An amide reacts with sodium hydroxide, bromine, and water to produce a primary amine and carbon dioxide."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_008.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00020",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00020-alt",
              "text": "An acyl chloride reacts with sodium azide and ethanol to form an intermediate. This reacts with water in the presence of heat to form an amine, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_009.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00021",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00021-alt",
              "text": "An alkyl amine reacts with methyl iodide, then silver oxide in the presence of heat to form an alkene."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_010.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00022",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00022-alt",
              "text": "Aniline reacts with nitrous acid and sulfuric acid to form an arene diazonium salt and H S O 4 minus."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_011.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00023",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00023-alt",
              "text": "Benezenediazonium hydrogen phosphate can react with any of six different sets of reagents to form chlorobenzene, bromobenzene, iodobenzene, cyanobenzene, phenol and benzene."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_012.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00024",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00298:alt:fig-00024-alt",
              "text": "Benzenediazonium hydrogen phosphate reacts with phenol to form para-hydroxyazobenzene. It reacts with aryl amine to form para-aminoazobenzene in which a secondary amine is bonded at fourth position."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_EC_013.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
